\newcommand{\sq}{\sigma}
\title{A Latin square in the bipartite complement}
\author{Human Compression project}
\date{}
\begin{document}\maketitle
For an edge $xy$ of a triangle-free graph, write $\sq(xy)$ for its
number of incident squares. This paper proves the following two
entries of Table~4 in Goedgebeur and Jooken~\cite{GJ}. One finite
exclusion is established by a complete search with a prescribed
five-cycle; the bipartite classification is proved directly.

\begin{theorem}\label{main}
There is exactly one simple graph, up to isomorphism, with eighteen
vertices, degree six, girth four and fourteen squares through every
edge. It is the bipartite complement of the incidence graph of the
transversal design obtained from the Latin square of order three.
Moreover
\[
                 n(6,4,10)\ge20,
\]
where $n(k,4,\lambda)$ is the least order of a graph of degree $k$,
girth four and $\lambda$ squares through each edge, or infinity if
no such graph exists.
\end{theorem}

\begin{lemma}\label{bip}
Let $G$ be a bipartite graph with eighteen vertices, degree six and
constant square count $\lambda$. Then $\lambda\ge14$. If
$\lambda=14$, its bipartite complement is the incidence graph of a
transversal design with three groups of size three, unique up to
isomorphism.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Regularity gives two parts $P,Q$ of size nine. Let $H$ be the
complement of $G$ in $K_{P,Q}$; it has degree three. Fix $x\in P$,
and for $t\in P\setminus\{x\}$ put
$\mu(x,t)=|N_H(x)\cap N_H(t)|$. The common neighbourhood in $G$
has size $3+\mu(x,t)$, and
$\sum_{t\ne x}\mu(x,t)=3\cdot2=6$. For an edge $xy$ of $G$,
counting its squares by the other vertex in $P$ gives
\begin{equation}\label{defect}
 \lambda=\sum_{t\in N_G(y)\setminus\{x\}}(2+\mu(x,t))
        =16-p_H(x,y),
\end{equation}
where $p_H(x,y)$ is the number of walks of length three from $x$
to $y$ in $H$. Thus this number has a common value
$\delta=16-\lambda$ for the six vertices $y\in Q\setminus N_H(x)$.

There are $3^3=27$ walks of length three starting at $x$.
For each $y\in N_H(x)$, exactly five of those ending at $y$
backtrack: three have the form $x-u-x-y$ and three have the form $x-y-v-y$,
with $x-y-x-y$ counted in both lists. Every other such walk
corresponds to a square through the edge $xy$ of $H$.
If $c(x)$ is the number of squares of $H$ through $x$, it follows
that
\[
               27=15+2c(x)+6\delta.
\]
In particular $\delta\le2$ and $\lambda\ge14$.
Equality implies $c(x)=0$ for every $x$, so $H$ has girth at least six.

Regard $P$ as nine points and $Q$ as nine lines, with incidence given
by $H$. Every line has three points, and every point is on three
lines. No pair of points lies on two lines, because there is no
quadrangle. A point $x$ is therefore collinear with exactly six
other points, and noncollinear with exactly two, say $y,z$.
For each line $L$ not through $x$, the value $p_H(x,L)=2$ counts
the points on $L$ collinear with $x$: each such point contributes
its unique joining line. Consequently exactly one point of $L$
is noncollinear with $x$.

If $y,z$ were on a line, that line would not contain $x$ and would
have two points noncollinear with $x$, a contradiction. Hence
$\{x,y,z\}$ is a set of three mutually noncollinear points.
Each of its members has the other two as its complete set of
noncollinear points. These triples consequently partition $P$
into three groups of size three. Every line meets each group
once, and every pair from different groups lies on a unique line.

Label the groups $\{a_0,a_1,a_2\}$, $\{b_0,b_1,b_2\}$,
$\{c_0,c_1,c_2\}$. The line through $a_i,b_j$ meets the third
group in $c_{L(i,j)}$. The array $L$ is a Latin square: a repeated
entry in a row or column would put a point pair on two lines.
Normalize its first row and column to $0,1,2$ by relabelling.
Its central entry cannot be $1$. If it were $0$, the next entry
of that row would be $2$, repeating the top entry of the same
column. Thus it is $2$, and all remaining entries are forced:
\[
                   L=\begin{pmatrix}0&1&2\\1&2&0\\2&0&1\end{pmatrix}.
\]
This proves uniqueness of $H$ and of its bipartite complement.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Finite exclusion]\label{finite}
There is no nonbipartite simple triangle-free graph on eighteen
vertices, of degree six, with constant square count in
$\{10,12,14\}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
We give the complete reduction and the exact search used for its
remaining finite part. The shortest-odd-cycle inequality
$k\ell\le2n$, proved in the preceding component~\cite{order16},
forces a five-cycle, since $6\ell\le36$ and $\ell$ is odd and at
least five. Label this cycle $v_0,\ldots,v_4$, with indices modulo
five. A vertex has at most two neighbours on it, and such a pair
must be nonconsecutive. As before define
\[
 A_i=\{x:N(x)\cap V(C)=\{v_{i-1},v_{i+1}\}\},\quad
 B_i=\{x:N(x)\cap V(C)=\{v_i\}\},
\]
and let $Z$ contain vertices with no cycle neighbour. Here $v_i\in A_i$.
For $a_i=|A_i|$, $b_i=|B_i|$, $z=|Z|$, the exact degree equations are
\begin{equation}\label{sizes}
 a_{i-1}+a_{i+1}+b_i=6,\qquad
 \sum_i b_i+2z=6,\qquad a_i\ge1,\ b_i,z\ge0.
\end{equation}
The second equality follows by subtracting the cycle-degree sum
from twice the total order. In particular $0\le z\le3$.

For every $z$, enumerate the weak compositions of $6-2z$ into
five entries $b_i$. These exhaust all possibilities. Put $c_i=6-b_i$.
The unique solution for the $a_i$ is obtained from
\[
 2a_0=c_0+c_1-c_2-c_3+c_4,\quad
 a_2=c_1-a_0,\quad a_3=c_4-a_0,\quad
 a_1=c_2-a_3,\quad a_4=c_0-a_1.
\]
The sum of the $b_i$ is even, so these values are integral.
Reject a solution with an $a_i<1$. Rotation and reversal of the
cycle permit replacing $b$ by the least of its ten dihedral images.
There remain twenty-seven class patterns. This reduction identifies
patterns only; it does not assume any further automorphism of a
graph with that pattern.

For each pattern assign distinct labels to all eighteen vertices,
with the five cycle vertices first, then the other vertices of each
$A_i$, the $B_i$, and $Z$. Insert precisely their prescribed edges
to the cycle. The cycle degrees are now six. All remaining edges
are between the thirteen other vertices. Initially a pair among
them is allowed unless its members share a cycle neighbour.
This is a necessary triangle condition, and imposes no further
restriction on a possible completion.

At a search node retain the chosen adjacency, the degree deficits,
and the set $U$ of vertices whose remaining neighbours are undecided.
For $x\in U$, let $T_x$ be the vertices $y\in U\setminus\{x\}$
of positive deficit such that $xy$ is allowed and the present
neighbourhoods of $x,y$ are disjoint. If $|T_x|$ is smaller than
the deficit of $x$, reject the node. Otherwise choose one $x$
deterministically, and branch over every subset of $T_x$ of size
equal to its deficit. Reject a chosen subset containing a present
edge, since its endpoints with $x$ would form a triangle. Insert
all selected edges, subtract the corresponding deficits, and
remove $x$ from $U$. Backtracking restores all entries exactly.

This enumerates every triangle-free regular completion. Indeed,
in any completion extending the present state, the unchosen
neighbours of the selected $x$ form exactly one of the subsets
just described. No neighbour can be among an earlier removed
vertex, whose degree was already closed. The necessary degree
and triangle rejections cannot remove that subset. Induction on
$|U|$ therefore proves completeness. Conversely, when $U$ is
empty all degrees are six, and every new edge was tested for
triangles, so every retained completion has the stated properties.

The implementation uses one further necessary bound. The two
opposite neighbourhoods of each original cycle edge are already
fixed. Count their presently chosen cross edges, and add all
currently possible unchosen cross edges with both endpoints in
$U$ and one endpoint in the other's $T_x$. The present count is
a lower bound and this sum is an upper bound for its final square
count. Delete a target value outside either bound; reject a node
only when no target in $\{10,12,14\}$ remains compatible with all
five cycle edges. An upper bound may include mutually incompatible
future edges, which can only weaken this rejection. At a complete
graph the program counts squares on every edge, not just on the
chosen cycle, and retains it only if the count is constant and
belongs to the target set.

The attached exact program completes all twenty-seven patterns,
visiting $772\,757$ recursion nodes, and retains no graph.
It uses labelled adjacency bit sets and integer counts; it has no
numerical tolerance, group-library equivalence test, or borrowed
graph catalogue. The detailed records give the class sizes and
completed search for every pattern. Together with the exhaustive
reduction and branching argument above, these computations prove
the finite exclusion.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{main}]
The finite exclusion makes every graph with the first parameters
bipartite, so Lemma~\ref{bip} proves uniqueness. For existence, take
the nine points and nine lines of the displayed Latin square.
Its incidence graph $H$ has degree three, no quadrangle, and for
each nonincident point and line exactly two length-three paths.
Its bipartite complement therefore has degree six and, by
\eqref{defect}, fourteen squares on every edge. It is connected:
any two vertices in the same part have at least three common
neighbours, and every vertex has positive degree. Its girth is four.

For the lower bound at $\lambda=10$, a triangle-free graph of degree
six has $n\ge12$, and counting incidences with squares gives
$8\mid6n\lambda$. The candidate orders below twenty are therefore
$12,14,16,18$. At twelve and fourteen the shortest-odd-cycle
inequality forces bipartiteness. The graphs are respectively $K_{6,6}$
and $K_{7,7}$ with a perfect matching removed, whose square counts
are $25$ and $20$. The preceding order-sixteen classification
\cite{order16} allows only $\lambda=17$. At eighteen,
Lemmas~\ref{bip} and~\ref{finite} exclude $\lambda=10$.
This proves the required bound.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Certificates and limits.}
The positive graph is checked by two square counts and a literal
isomorphism to the acquired source example. The finite search is
accompanied by a positive search at order fifteen, controls at order
sixteen, independent integer
enumeration of the cycle-class sizes, and examples of rejected
damaged graph certificates. Its separate exclusion of square count
twelve at order eighteen is also proved by Lemma~\ref{finite}.
This alone does not establish $n(6,4,12)\ge20$: the odd orders
seventeen and nineteen are not excluded by divisibility and are
outside the present accepted finite search.

\paragraph{Attribution.}
The exact count and the lower-bound target are prior results in
Table~4 of~\cite{GJ}, physical page~20. The earlier component
\cite{order16} supplies the shortest-odd-cycle inequality and the
order-sixteen classification; the cycle-neighbourhood partition is
reused. The present argument adds the complementary cubic incidence
geometry and an exact search confined to its nonbipartite alternative.
No historical priority or open-conjecture claim is made. The finite
part is a computational proof, not a Lean formalization.
\clearpage
